{"author_count":2,"contributors":["Gad, Christopher","Douglas-Jones, Rachel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381757"],"event":{"end":"2015-11-22","place":"Denver, Colorado, us","start":"2015-11-17","title":"Familiar Strange","year":"2015"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"computational thinking as an ontological programme","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-381757","ids":["itu-20dfff54-76e4-406a-a3b1-959cc5b5f72c"],"lang":"Danish","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-670CF3C80A8617911AE29230C2E49AC3"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"gadpau","last":"Gad","locid":"d55dab65-fffe-4da4-a383-cfcf5ee3a8cd","name":"Gad, Christopher","orcid":"0000-0002-1631-5483","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-20dfff54-76e4-406a-a3b1-959cc5b5f72c"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Computational Thinking as an Ontological Programme?","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381757","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"How does one conduct, measure and record a ‘good’ ethical review of biomedical research? To what extent do ethics committees invoke professionalism in researchers and in themselves, and to what extent do they see competence as adherence to a set of standard operating procedures for ethical review? Drawing on ethnographic fieldwork with the Forum of Ethics Review Committees of Asia and the Pacific (FERCAP), a capacity-building NGO that runs ethics committee trainings and reviews in the Asia Pacific region, I develop an analysis of ethical review and its effects. I focus on a ‘second-order audit’ run by FERCAP, which recognises committees according to a set of standards that are designed to render ‘local’ committees internationally legible. The article adds to a growing comparative literature that expands studies of audit-like measuring and disciplining activities beyond western contexts and enriches readings of ‘ethics’. I begin and end with a reflection on the ethical effects of a measurement practice that takes ethics itself as its object.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Douglas-Jones, Rachel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384933"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Asia","Audit","Professions","Research ethics","Transnational"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a good ethical review audit and professionalism in research ethics","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81821792/Douglas_Jones_2015.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384933","identifier":[{"id":"84923321107","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-f8d48ffe-77e4-4d1f-80e0-e9be849696ed"],"keyword":["Asia","audit","professions","research ethics","transnational"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-970F2C93F80083F8325060901756C736"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/81821792/Douglas_Jones_2015.pdf","version":"Authors Final Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2015","yearSub":"2015"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/1469-8676.12099","issn":["09640282","14698676"],"issn_org":["09640282"],"issue":"1","journal":"Social Anthropology","pages":"53-67","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Social Anthropology","volume":"23"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84923321107"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-f8d48ffe-77e4-4d1f-80e0-e9be849696ed"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A 'good' ethical review: audit and professionalism in research ethics","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384933","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Proponents of computational thinking use the concept to account for what they perceive as important generalizable aspects of human thought (Wing 2011, National Research Council USA 2010, 2011). Simultaneously, the concept is employed to designate an ambitious pedagogical programme, in which computational thinking can be taught as a skill for the digitally literate 21st century (ibid.). As such, CT is seen both as an innate human capacity and a programme for developing future oriented skills - both for individuals and for populations at large. This paper explores what we perceive as conceptual slippage within the computational thinking concept, as it moves between the descriptive and promotional modes described above. We consider the implications of this slippage through various conceptual apparatuses available within STS – since these approaches are already critical of distinctions between the innate vs the learned, the normative and the descriptive. This exercise sets out the ground for considering CT – its promises and perils - as a growing arena for research in which the analytic techniques of STS are both needed and challenged.","author_count":2,"contributors":["Gad, Christopher","Douglas-Jones, Rachel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384525"],"event":{"end":"2015-05-29","place":"Copenhagen, dk","start":"2015-05-27","title":"2nd Nordic Science and Technology Studies (STS) Conference","year":"2015"},"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Computational thinking","Conceptual Slippage","Digital literacy","Pedagogy","Science and Technology Studies STS"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Undetermined","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"promises and perils of computational thinking a discussion piece","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dca","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"id":"S01-000.00-384525","ids":["itu-ddc571f9-c881-4bba-b01e-ec330f52979d"],"keyword":["Computational Thinking","Conceptual Slippage","Digital Literacy","Pedagogy","Science and Technology Studies (STS)"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-FFD542C076E22E3A57F151C299E3A061"],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Christopher","key":"gadpau","last":"Gad","locid":"d55dab65-fffe-4da4-a383-cfcf5ee3a8cd","name":"Gad, Christopher","orcid":"0000-0002-1631-5483","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-ddc571f9-c881-4bba-b01e-ec330f52979d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Promises and perils of computational thinking : A discussion piece","type":"Conference Abstract","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384525","year":"2015","yearsub":"2015"}
{"abstract":"Drawing on interdisciplinary research focusing on Durham University estate, we describe how buildings constructed as part of an eighteenth century transition to a high carbon coal-based economy, are used and understood by their current inhabitants. Applied heritage research has tended to focus on the thermal and energetic properties of historic buildings, as distinct from their social meaning and use. A similar separation between the physi- cal building and its social use is inherent in methodologies such as energy audits that constitute key devices through which buildings are institution- ally managed. We argue that these perspectives have overlooked how a sig- nificant element of energy use arises from the complex practical interactions between people and infrastructure. From this perspective we argue that better outcomes for energy and heritage would result if greater contextual consideration was given to the existing possibilities afforded by historic buildings and their users","author_count":5,"contributors":["Douglas-Jones, Rachel","Adams, Charlotte","Green, Adrian","Lewis, Quentin","Yarrow, Thomas"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382540"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"country":["United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","Durham University"],"keyword":["Anthropology","Archaeology","Audits","Buildings","Energy","Engineering","Heritage","Retrofit"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"building with history exploring the relationship between heritage and energy in institutionally managed buildings","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1179/1756750514Z.00000000053","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382540","identifier":[{"id":"84923855590","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-550fc457-f015-4cc2-a7fd-1bb36649028a"],"keyword":["anthropology","archaeology","audits","buildings","energy","engineering","heritage","retrofit"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-4-2AC48B6F09121B2A3B43DBED5F8D06BA"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/79275789/1756750514Z_2E00000000053.pdf","version":"Other Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1179/1756750514Z.00000000053","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2014","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"gb","key":"durham university","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"Durham University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Charlotte","key":"adamspau","last":"Adams","name":"Adams, Charlotte","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Adrian","key":"greenpau","last":"Green","name":"Green, Adrian","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Quentin","key":"lewispau","last":"Lewis","name":"Lewis, Quentin","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Thomas","key":"yarrowpau","last":"Yarrow","name":"Yarrow, Thomas","rank":5,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1179/1756750514z.00000000053","issn":["17567505","17567513"],"issn_org":["17567505"],"issue":"2","journal":"The Historic Environment","pages":"167-81","publisher":"Routledge","status":"p","title":"The Historic Environment","volume":"5"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-84923855590"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-550fc457-f015-4cc2-a7fd-1bb36649028a"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Building with History : exploring the relationship between heritage and energy in institutionally managed buildings","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382540","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"It is widely recognised that the historic environment provides a source of cultural enrichment and enhances people’s quality of life and wellbeing. However, it also undergoes cycles of material transformation, of decay and renewal, which inform the meanings and values associated with it and contribute to the experience of authenticity. In this interdisciplinary project, we have used ethnographic methods including interviews and participant observation to examine the kinds of value attached to deterioration and decay in historic buildings. We use these methods firstly to ask how processes of deterioration and decay inform the cultural values attached to historic buildings, and secondly to explore when and why we use science-based interventions to retard or arrest these processes. Through case studies drawn from Scottish buildings in the care of National Trust Scotland and Historic Scotland, the study explores how these interventions impact on perceptions of authenticity and value. The project puts conversations with practitioners at the heart of the field of inquiry. It is oriented toward initiating discussion between research and practice, demonstrating the role of qualitative research and providing analysis that will inform the application of heritage science ot problems of material degradation and decay. ￼ The aim of the project workshop was to engage with stakeholders from conservation groups, practitioners, heritage scientists and academics from humanities and social disciplines to share the project results and to discuss the wider context and impact of this work. The summary that follows condenses the broad ranging discussion into some key points for reflection.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Douglas-Jones, Rachel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-384361"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Cultural Enrichment","Ethnographic methods","Heritage Science","Historic Environment","Material Transformation"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"nr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"materiality authenticity and value in the historic environment a study of the effects of material transformation and scientific intervention","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.uws.ac.uk/mavproject","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-384361","ids":["itu-d26f4ae8-d1a0-46f8-8ad4-9efdb78ba84d"],"keyword":["Cultural Enrichment","Ethnographic Methods","Heritage Science","Historic Environment","Material Transformation"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-5926FDF9CB9E893084B2C62C81940E54"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://www.uws.ac.uk/mavproject","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, IT management and leadership"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"status":"p"},"review":"No Review","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-d26f4ae8-d1a0-46f8-8ad4-9efdb78ba84d"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Materiality, Authenticity and Value in the historic environment : A study of the effects of material transformation and scientific intervention","type":"Report","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-384361","year":"2014","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"Development beyond politics is a book about the lives of activists, aid workers, and the social elite who run NGOs in Ghana. It is also a book about the lives of concepts, in the hands of these specific actors.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Douglas-Jones, Rachel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382481"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Development","Ghana","Governance"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"yarrow thomas development beyond politics aid activism and ngos in ghana","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"djr","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9655.12042_24","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382481","ids":["itu-50fe0c0f-7961-42cf-83a7-2059973d1482"],"keyword":["Ghana","development","governance"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-138892317DA90F903F7DE3D6F7CF3D7F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.1111/1467-9655.12042_24","version":"Other Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Unused","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2013","yearSub":"2014"},"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT","IT University of Copenhagen, Technologies in Practice, Technologies in Practice","The Department, Interdisciplinary Section"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.1111/1467-9655.12042_24","issn":["13590987","14679655"],"issn_org":["13590987"],"issue":"2","journal":"Journal of the Royal Anthropological Institute","pages":"429-430","publisher":"Wiley-Blackwell","status":"p","title":"Journal of the Royal Anthropological Institute","volume":"19"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-50fe0c0f-7961-42cf-83a7-2059973d1482"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Yarrow, Thomas. Development beyond politics: aid, activism and NGOs in Ghana.","type":"Journal Review","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382481","year":"2013","yearsub":"2014"}
{"abstract":"In exploring the work and role of ethics review committees in Asia, this paper takes as its starting point an image of a finely woven silk cloth. The image was used at an international conference to describe the ‘matrix’ of persons and institutions involved in biomedical research, with the implication that broken threads threatened the integrity of research endeavours. In what follows, I examine two points at which breaks in the cloth are perceived, and the work done to ‘bridge’ the resulting gaps. Drawing on fieldwork with a capacity building NGO that works to build the competence of research ethics committees in the Asian region, I show how the image can be used to understand the place of ethics as a contemporary concern in research settings.","author_count":1,"contributors":["Douglas-Jones, Rachel"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-382155"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["No Matches Found"],"author_count":"1 contributor","cluster_size":1,"keyword":["Asia","Capacity building","Research ethics","Trust"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"No","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"a single broken thread integrity trust and accountability in asian ethics committees","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://community.dur.ac.uk/anthropology.journal/vol18/iss2/douglas-jones2012.pdf","version":"Other Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-382155","ids":["itu-3c900633-82f9-4e14-814e-192fc3971625"],"keyword":["Asia","capacity building","research ethics","trust"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Local-1-1-F483E665723D45EB2D939C4202A2CA7F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","type":"rem","url":"http://community.dur.ac.uk/anthropology.journal/vol18/iss2/douglas-jones2012.pdf","version":"Other Version"}],"org":[{"affno":"1","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Business IT"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Rachel","key":"douglasjonespau","last":"Douglas-Jones","locid":"f11b0cc1-ad9f-4b12-b805-65b70e5bcd65","name":"Douglas-Jones, Rachel","orcid":"0000-0001-9562-6572","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"issn":["17422930"],"issue":"2","journal":"Durham Anthropology Journal","publisher":"Durham University","status":"p","title":"Durham Anthropology Journal","volume":"18"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-3c900633-82f9-4e14-814e-192fc3971625"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"A single broken thread : Integrity, trust and accountability in Asian ethics committees","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382155","year":"2012","yearsub":"2012"}
